Secure SSO with MFA Integration – Technical Specification
Overview
The banking application requires integration with the organization’s OAuth-based Single Sign-On (SSO) with Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA). The implementation must ensure secure handling of JWT tokens and user details, preventing data loss and vulnerabilities.

Authentication Flow
1. User Accesses Application:
If an active session exists, redirect the user to the home page (/home).
If no active session, redirect the user to the organization’s SSO login page via the well-known API.
2. SSO Authentication & MFA Process:
The user authenticates with credentials & MFA on the organization’s login page.
Upon successful authentication, the organization redirects to the application's callback URL with a JWT token.
3. Token Processing & Session Creation:
The Next.js frontend (using NextAuth.js with a custom provider) extracts the JWT token securely.
Instead of direct client-side extraction, the Java Spring Boot API extracts and validates the JWT to enhance security.
User details (roles, permissions, token expiration, etc.) are extracted securely from the JWT payload.
A secure session is established using Redux Toolkit for state management.
4. Token Storage & Security Considerations:
Store the JWT token only in HTTP-only, Secure, and SameSite-Strict cookies to prevent XSS attacks.
Do not store JWT in localStorage/sessionStorage.
Implement a refresh token mechanism to ensure session continuity securely.
Implement Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) to restrict route access based on user roles.
